{"toolCallId":"call_d4ca1b70f52c48d696d2c2f976823808","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8a66e200e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"[2-3 sentences: what this implements and the technical approach]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n<!-- FILL: Fill the stack row and write three sentences: what changes, where, and how you will prove it. GOOD: Change one bash script. Add one output branch. Prove it with a line count. WEAK: This plan describes the implementation. -->\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., TypeScript, Python 3.11] |\n| **Framework** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., React, FastAPI] |\n| **Storage** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., PostgreSQL, None] |\n| **Testing** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., Jest, pytest] |\n\n### Overview\n[2-3 sentences: what this implements and the technical approach]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n<!-- FILL: Turn each gate into a check a command can answer. Keep the boxes unticked until they pass. GOOD: `rg -n 'quiet' recommend-level.sh` prints the new branch before the task starts. WEAK: The team agrees the plan is ready. -->\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [ ] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [ ] Success criteria measurable\n- [ ] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n<!-- FILL: Name the pattern in three words, then list the one component that changes. GOOD: Single script branch. The component is `recommend-level.sh`. WEAK: Standard architecture. -->\n\n### Pattern\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: MVC | MVVM | Clean Architecture | Serverless | Monolith | Other]\n\n### Key Components\n- **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Component 1]**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Purpose]\n- **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Component 2]**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Purpose]\n\n### Data Flow\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description of how data moves through the system]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n<!-- FILL: Point to `tasks.md` and name the three phases in one line. GOOD: Setup reads the current output. Implementation adds the branch. Verification counts lines. WEAK: See tasks. -->\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n<!-- FILL: Name the one command you will run and the output it must print. GOOD: `--quiet` prints 1 line. The default prints 17. WEAK: Test it manually. -->\n\nN/A — record any testing beyond the verification tasks in `tasks.md` here.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: List each tool the change needs, or write `None`. GOOD: `bash` and `awk`. No new package. WEAK: Usual dependencies. -->\n\nN/A — record dependencies beyond the components named in the architecture here.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n<!-- FILL: Name the trigger and the one command that reverts the change. GOOD: Trigger: the default output changes. Revert the one commit. WEAK: Roll back if needed. -->\n\nN/A — record rollback steps beyond reverting the scoped change here.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"Add one option branch and one exit-status gate to a single bash script. The report stays byte-identical; only the trailing exit code learns a threshold.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer with `bc`, both already required by the script |\n| **Framework** | None - a standalone CLI script under `runtime/cli/spec/` |\n| **Storage** | None; per-file statistics live in a temporary directory removed by the existing `trap` |\n| **Testing** | Manual invocation with `diff` between runs, plus the strict spec validator |\n\n### Overview\nThe change adds one case to the argument loop in section 8 of `calculate-completeness.sh` and one comparison between the report emitters and the trailing `exit 0`. The comparison reuses the percentage the report already computed, so no counting, formatting or output code is touched. Proof is a `diff` of the report with and without the flag, plus a check of the exit status at the boundary and on both sides of it.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-script option branch\n\n### Key Components\n- **Argument loop (section 8)**: takes `--min-percent` with its value, validates it, and stores it in a new global alongside `VERBOSE`, `JSON_OUTPUT` and `QUALITY_CHECK`.\n- **Report emitters (`output_text`, `output_json`)**: unchanged. They keep sole ownership of what the report says.\n- **Exit-status gate (end of section 8)**: reads the completion figure the report just printed and turns it into the process status.\n\n### Data Flow\n1. The argument loop reads `--min-percent <N>` and rejects a missing or out-of-range value before any work starts.\n2. The existing calculation runs untouched and sets `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` in folder mode, or the per-file `percentage` in `--file` mode.\n3. The existing emitter prints the report exactly as it does today.\n4. The gate compares the same figure the emitter used against `N`, then exits 3 when it is below `N` and 0 otherwise.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. Setup captures the current report and exit path. Implementation adds the option branch and the exit gate. Verification diffs the report and exercises every exit code.\n\n### Step 1: Parse and validate the flag\n\nAcceptance: `--min-percent` with a valid value is accepted, and a missing or invalid value exits 1 before any calculation runs.\n\nVerify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent abc specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent; echo $?` prints one `ERROR:` line and `1`.\n\n### Step 2: Gate the exit status on the reported percentage\n\nAcceptance: the script exits 3 when the reported completion is below `N` and 0 when it is at or above `N`, with the report printed in full before either status.\n\nVerify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 101 specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent` is rejected as invalid, while `--min-percent 0` prints the full report and exits 0.\n\n### Step 3: Document the flag and prove the report is unchanged\n\nAcceptance: `--help` lists the option, and a `diff` of the report with and without the flag is empty in text and JSON mode.\n\nVerify: `diff before.txt after.txt` prints nothing, and `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh --help` prints a line containing `--min-percent`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Regression | Text and JSON reports, folder and `--file` mode, with and without the flag | `diff` between captured runs |\n| Boundary | `N` equal to the reported percentage, one below and one above | `echo $?` after each invocation |\n| Error path | Missing value, non-integer, negative and out-of-range `N` | `echo $?` plus the stderr line |\n| Packet | The planning documents themselves | `validate.sh --strict` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Runtime | Green | The script does not run at all today; no change |\n| `bc` | Runtime | Green | The existing percentage math already fails without it |\n| `diff` | Verification only | Green | Compare the captured reports by eye instead |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The report differs between a run with and without the flag, or a run without the flag stops exiting 0.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touches `calculate-completeness.sh`. The change adds no files, no configuration and no state, so the revert restores the previous behavior completely.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":49,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":26}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8a66e200e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_dbc41e6b0d9c4602af4e093d382a6ba7","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8a66e200e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent\"\n  - \"add a min percent option to calculate completeness\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n<!-- FILL: Fill every row: level, priority, status, date and branch. Use the real branch name. GOOD: Priority P1. Status Draft. Branch `feat/recommend-level-quiet`. WEAK: Priority TBD. Status maybe. -->\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 1 |\n| **Priority** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-completeness-min-percent` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n<!-- FILL: State the problem in two sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How to verify it's done] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How to verify it's done] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 7. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` also suppress warnings on stderr? Decision: no, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"calculate-completeness.sh reports a completion percentage but always exits 0, so a caller cannot gate on a threshold without parsing stdout. This spec adds a --min-percent <N> flag that turns the reported percentage into an exit status while leaving the report untouched.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent\"\n  - \"add a min percent option to calculate completeness\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 1 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `detached HEAD` (packet created with `--skip-branch`) |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`calculate-completeness.sh` prints `Overall Completion: <N>%` and then exits 0 on every successful run, whatever the percentage. A caller that wants to fail a build or a gate below a completeness floor has to parse stdout, which breaks when the label, spacing or status line changes, and which reads a different key in `--json` mode.\n\n### Purpose\nLet a caller enforce a completeness floor with one flag and one exit status, so the report stays a report and the pass/fail decision moves into the shell.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add `--min-percent <N>` to the argument loop in section 8 of the script.\n- Validate `N` as an integer from 0 to 100, and exit 1 with a stderr message for a missing or invalid value.\n- Compare the completion figure the report already prints against `N` after the report is emitted, exiting 3 when it is below `N` and 0 otherwise.\n- Document the option in the `--help` text.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- Changing the completion math, the quality thresholds or the report text - the report must stay byte-identical, so the flag may only affect the exit status.\n- Adding a short alias such as `-m` - the request names the long form only, and an alias can follow when a caller asks for one.\n- Folding `--quality` warnings into the threshold - those warnings stay advisory and do not change the exit status.\n- Touching `validate.sh` or any other script in the directory - the change is confined to this one file.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh` | Modify | Parse `--min-percent <N>`, document it in `--help`, and gate the trailing exit status on the reported completion |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--min-percent <N>` is accepted as a long option that takes one integer argument in the range 0 to 100. | `bash calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 80 <folder>` runs to completion and exits with a status derived from the report. |\n| REQ-002 | The script exits 3 when the reported completion is below `N`, and exits 0 when it is equal to or above `N`. | On a folder whose report reads `Overall Completion: 40%`, `--min-percent 80` exits 3, while `--min-percent 40` and `--min-percent 10` exit 0. |\n| REQ-003 | A missing or invalid `N` exits 1 with an `ERROR:` line on stderr, matching the script's existing usage-error path. | `--min-percent abc`, `--min-percent 101`, `--min-percent -1` and a bare `--min-percent` each exit 1 and print one `ERROR:` line to stderr. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-004 | The completeness report on stdout is byte-identical with and without the flag, in text and JSON mode, for both folder and `--file` invocations. | `diff` between the two runs is empty and both files end at the same line count. |\n| REQ-005 | `--help` lists the new option next to the existing ones. | `bash calculate-completeness.sh --help` prints one line containing `--min-percent`. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: A caller can gate on a completeness floor with `--min-percent <N>` and read the answer from `$?` alone, with no stdout parsing.\n- **SC-002**: **Given** a folder whose report reads `Overall Completion: 40%`, **When** the script runs with `--min-percent 80`, **Then** it exits 3 and prints exactly the same report as a run without the flag.\n- **SC-003**: **Given** the same folder, **When** the script runs with `--min-percent 40`, **Then** it exits 0 because 40 is not below 40.\n- **SC-004**: Exit codes 0 and 1 keep the meanings they have today, and a run without the flag exits 0 as it does now.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` 3.2 or newer and `bc`, both already required by the script | The flag cannot run where the script cannot run today | No new dependency; the comparison is builtin integer arithmetic |\n| Risk | A bare `[[ ... ]] && exit 3` under `set -e` leaves a non-zero status on the false branch and changes a passing run's exit code | High | Write the gate as an explicit `if` block, the same shape the percentage clamp already uses |\n| Risk | An existing caller treats exit 0 as \"the script ran\" and now sees 3 | Medium | Exit 3 only when the flag is present; a run without it keeps exit 0 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 7. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does the threshold apply in `--file` mode, where the report prints `Completion:` rather than `Overall Completion:`? Decision: yes, compare the per-file `Completion` figure, the only completion number that mode reports.\n- Does a missing target count as 0%? Decision: yes, a `Status: NOT FOUND` file reports a completion of 0, so the flag exits 3 for any `N` above 0.\n- Should `N` accept a decimal such as `85.5`? Decision: no, the report rounds to whole percent, so integer comparison matches the number the caller sees.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":29,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":28}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8a66e200e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
